\small $^\ast$ Single seed; seed2 best checkpoint unavailable for re-evaluation.
\end{flushleft}
% exp_id: hsweep_h2_seed1, factorial_h2_episodeadv_seed1, exp029_episode_level, exp029_seed3_v2
\end{table}

\paragraph{Additional conditions.} Dense reward, REINFORCE++, and discount-factor sweep experiments all confirm that sign misattribution, not reward sparsity or discounting, drives the failure (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

\paragraph{Window-size sweep.} Varying window size reveals $h{=}10$ as a seed-dependent boundary (Figure~\ref{fig:hsweep}b). Windows of $h{\leq}5$ collapse to 0\% across all seeds; $h{=}20$ and full-episode conditions recover monotonically. At $h{=}10$, two seeds diverge (6.25\% vs.\ 21.9\%, mean 14.1\%), placing this window size at the instability boundary rather than in a deterministic collapse zone. All other conditions are single-seed.% exp_id: h_sweep_series, exp029_seed1, hsweep_h10_seed2_retry2
\label{sec:results_hsweep}

\subsection{Sign Misattribution Verification}
\label{sec:sign_verification}

We distinguish three metrics that each capture a different aspect of credit assignment quality:

\textit{(1) Sign-episode correlation}: whether advantage sign tracks episode outcome. Under binary reward (penalty${=}0$), correlation is near-perfect at 99.6\% ($270/271$ non-zero windows; Figure~\ref{fig:sign_misattribution}), directly confirming M2: advantage sign is determined by episode outcome, not local action quality.\footnote{The single discordant window arises from GRPO group-composition effects, not a violation of the mechanism.} This follows by construction, since all windows in a successful episode share the same positive return.% exp_id: binary_credit_agreement_iter0

\textit{(2) Credit agreement rate}: whether advantage sign matches local action quality, operationalized via action validity as a proxy (Definition~1). Under penalty reward ($h{=}2$, $n{=}3{,}000$ windows), only 52.07\% of valid-action windows receive positive advantage ($p{=}0.025$ vs.\ 50\%; Cohen's $h \approx 0.041$, below the small-effect threshold), confirming near-random credit agreement. Under binary reward, agreement drops to 28\% ($n{=}271$)---advantage sign systematically penalizes valid actions in failed episodes. This metric is an upper bound: valid actions include strategically suboptimal moves, yet agreement remains near-random even under this generous criterion.% exp_id: 7b_cell_a_h2

\textit{(3) $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update})$}: the theoretical upper bound from Proposition~2 ($\leq$19.1\% at $p{=}12.5\%$, $K{=}8$). This bounds the fraction of non-zero gradient updates with correct sign, a different quantity from the credit agreement rate, which conditions on action validity rather than gradient participation. The two metrics are complementary: Proposition~2 bounds worst-case gradient quality; credit agreement measures empirical signal strength.

The empirical magnitude ratio $|\hat{A}_{\text{correct}}|/|\hat{A}_{\text{wrong}}| \approx 2.6$ at step~5 ($n{=}30$) falls below the break-even threshold $(1{-}P_c)/P_c \approx 4.2$, confirming magnitude asymmetry cannot compensate for sign frequency (Section~\ref{sec:theory}). Combined with $\gamma{=}1.0$ collapse (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}), these measurements support sign misattribution as a primary factor, though the credit agreement effect is small ($+$2pp).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_sign_misattribution.pdf}
\caption{Sign-episode correlation (binary reward) vs.\ credit agreement rate (penalty reward). Under binary reward, advantage sign tracks episode outcome near-perfectly, confirming M2. Under penalty reward, credit agreement drops to near-random chance (dashed line).}
\label{fig:sign_misattribution}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Episode-Level Training: Seed Variability}
\label{sec:seed_variability}

Episode-level GRPO addresses the dual-mechanism failure, but outcomes remain seed-dependent. We evaluate $N{=}11$ seeds under identical hyperparameters, classifying each as \textbf{collapsed} (in-distribution success declines $>$50\% from peak for $\geq$3 steps), \textbf{stagnant} (stable but not significant vs.\ SFT), or \textbf{generalized} (Fisher exact $p{<}0.01$ vs.\ SFT on the 134-task OOD set). Table~\ref{tab:seed_variability} reports best-checkpoint results per seed.

